sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Among all possible rules of a given type (such as cellular automata) whose behavior is not obviously simple, i.e. not repetitive or nested, the overwhelming majority are computationally universal, so universality is extremely common rather than a rare engineered property.";
    dc:type "mathematical";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "An overwhelming fraction of non-simple rules are universal";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “The Content of the Principle” (from p. 719), p. 721. Scope: enumerable classes of simple programs such as cellular automata, Turing machines and related systems"
}
